---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

export const meta = {
  service: "instrumentation",
  image: "/case-studies/field-instrumentation/early-prototype.jpg",
  title: "A satellite modem for landslide early warning",
  blurb:
    "Extreme rainfall that triggers landslides is missed by weather models and falls beyond cell coverage. Developing NB-IoT-via-satellite telemetry to make dense, remote rain-gauge networks affordable.",
};
---

<Layout
  title="Case study: Developing a remote satellite modem for landslide prevention"
  description={meta.blurb}
>
  <article>
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study · Field instrumentation · In development</p>
    <h1>Measuring the rain that models miss — anywhere</h1>

    <p>
      Extreme rainfall events are often missed by even the most advanced
      numerical models — the only way to catch them is to measure on site.
      Worse, the rain that matters tends to fall far from any connectivity,
      so getting the data out is the hard part.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/case-studies/field-instrumentation/landslide_threshold.svg"
        alt="Rainfall intensity versus 96-hour accumulated rain for the February 2023 São Sebastião landslide event: only the on-site rain gauge crosses the Tatizana landslide-threshold envelopes; ERA5-Land, GFS and MERGE stay below them"
        loading="lazy"
        width="860"
        height="660"
      />
      <figcaption>
        A recent landslide event (São Sebastião, Brazil, February 2023) seen
        by an on-site rain gauge versus gridded rainfall products: only the
        gauge (blue) crosses the intensity&ndash;accumulation thresholds used
        for landslide warning — the models would have stayed silent.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/case-studies/field-instrumentation/cumulative.svg"
        alt="Cumulative rainfall curves at two stations during an extreme event: the rain gauge accumulates far more rain than the GFS and ERA5-Land gridded products"
        loading="lazy"
        width="950"
        height="430"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The same blind spot in accumulated rainfall: through the event, the
        gauges (blue) consistently outpace the gridded products.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      I am developing the next generation of modems for environmental
      applications, built on the new 3GPP NTN standard — NB-IoT via
      satellite. It slashes the hardware requirements and cost of satellite
      communication, making it viable to deploy rain gauges and other sensors
      far beyond cell coverage — and to build the dense monitoring networks
      that landslide early warning needs.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/case-studies/field-instrumentation/early-prototype.jpg"
        alt="Early prototype of the monitoring station: a tipping-bucket rain gauge on a 3D-printed mount, and its solar panel in a 3D-printed frame"
        loading="lazy"
        width="1692"
        height="1400"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The early prototype on test: tipping-bucket rain gauge and solar
        power, on 3D-printed mounts.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .eyebrow {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  figure {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  figure img {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 65vh;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    /* the SVG figures use light text — they need a dark backdrop in both themes */
    background: #0f172a;
  }
  figcaption {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
